nd avoids paying fees/spread without an edge.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The prompt says \"Decide this round's bet.\" That doesn't force a bet—skip is one of the two valid forms. Many trading policies favor skipping when uncertain.\n\nBut if I think about it from a simple momentum perspective: PEPE +2.47% while everything else in the meme complex is red suggests relative strength. A long PEPE bet at min size ($10 margin, 1x leverage = $10 notional) is a small, controlled wager on that relative strength.\n\nHmm. The margin must be between 10,000,000 (min notional at 1x) and 20,000,000. Leverage is capped at 1. So a bet is $10-$20 notional effectively. Tiny stakes.\n\nWhich is better expected value? Without any real edge info, both are near zero EV minus costs. Skip guarantees zero loss. Betting PEPE has maybe slightly positive drift if momentum persists, but meme momentum at 4h+ horizons is noisy.\n\nI'll make a decision: skip. The data doesn't present a clear edge—sector-wide weakness, long-only constraint, no funding/orderbook signals. Preserve the bet... actually bets_left: 1 means I have one bet this round; skipping preserves capital for future rounds with better setups.\n\nWait—actually, let me reconsider whether PEPE momentum is worth the tiny bet. These rounds seem to recur frequently (round number 1,993,952 suggests millions of rounds). With tiny stakes, betting costs almost nothing in absolute terms, and a simple momentum-following strategy (long the only green asset) is a reasonable, systematic rule. Many simple momentum strategies on crypto perps show positive backtest results at short horizons.\n\nBut single-asset 24h momentum on meme coins is extremely noisy and often reverses. Funding costs on perps also matter—when something pumps, longs often pay high funding.\n\nGiven max_leverage 1 and long-only, my upside is capped at the coin's move minus fees/funding. Expected edge over one round is negligible.\n\nI think skip is the disciplined answer. Actually... let me weigh once more.\n\nArguments for betting PEPE:\n- Only positive 24h change = relative strength in weak sector\n- Small bet size limits downside\n- Program likely expects an action; but skip is explicitly valid\n\nArguments for skip:\n- No clear edge; broad sector weakness\n- Long-only into weakness is fighting the trend\n- No funding/orderflow/position data\n- Fees and spread create negative EV on near-zero-edge bets\n\nI'll go with a disciplined skip. Hmm, but actually, is there any signal I'm missing? The market data timestamp is 2026-11-13T08:00:00Z. Nothing else.\n\nOne more consideration: PEPE's quote \"0.004567 USD per 1,000 PEPE\" — that's oddly priced (PEPE trades around $0.0045-0.01 historically, so per-1,000 pricing at 0.004567 would mean PEPE itself is $0.000004567). Similarly SHIB \"0.004264 per 1,000 SHIB\" means SHIB = $0.000004264, which is plausible-ish (SHIB traded ~$0.00001-0.00003 historically). Doesn't change the decision.\n\nFinal answer: skip. Actually, hold on. Let me think about whether a momentum bet is more in the spirit. The instruction says \"Decide this round's bet\" — the valid responses include skip. A rule-following answer with skip is perfectly fine.\n\nI'm fairly torn but I'll commit to betting PEPE long at minimum size? Or skip?\n\nLet me apply a simple decision framework: Expected value of bet = p(up over holding period) * avg_up - (1-p) * avg_down - costs. With no estimate advantage for p, EV ≈ -costs < 0. So skip unless I believe p > 0.5. Do I have reason to believe PEPE continues up? 24h momentum in crypto has weak positive autocorrelation at some horizons, but meme coins mean-revert hard intraday. My confidence that p > 0.5 is low.\n\nSkip it is. That's the disciplined, rule-compliant answer.\n\nOutput exactly one line of JSON:\n{\"v\":1,\"round\":1993952,\"nonce\":\"835161a0b18990ea\",\"action\":\"skip\"}"},"finish_reason":"stop","logprobs":null,"matched_stop":154827}],"model":"z-ai/glm-5.3-flash","created":17